{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The remaining gap asked what the durable term regression does at cluster level, and this turn answers it conditionally from the two source paths that decide it rather than spending an execution on a sequence whose outcome those paths already fix. A node that has gone backwards sends a lower term than its peers hold: appendEntries returns immediately when the request term is below the receiver's current term, and a vote request with an older term returns before anything else is considered, so against peers at the term it had adopted the regressed node can neither replicate nor win an election. The repair is the same path read the other way: an appendEntries whose term is above the receiver's makes it a follower and stores that higher term, so the first heartbeat from such a peer restores it and the inconsistency is transient. The consequence is therefore conditional on reachability rather than uniformly harmful: with a higher-term peer reachable the regression self-corrects within a heartbeat interval, and while the node is isolated - the state the single-node witness already exercised, where it went on to hold leadership at the lower term - its decisions rest on a term basis the cluster has abandoned, and because setCurrentTerm persists that value a restart while isolated resumes there. I also checked the harm that would turn this into a safety claim and it does not hold: the vote record is keyed by term and the duplicate check refuses a different candidate in a term the node has already voted in, so revisiting an earlier term cannot produce a second vote; what the regression removes is the node's knowledge that it had seen a higher term, which is the observation Raft leans on to keep stale candidates from campaigning as though nothing had happened. I recorded the conditional interpretation with its two new source ranges, answered the gap with the condition stated, and kept the claim at the regression already witnessed rather than inflating it to a safety violation. The cluster-level half remains a prediction from those paths and is noted as such, since no multi-node execution has yet shown a peer ignoring the regressed node's messages. The other witnessed findings remain recorded and unaffected.\"}"}}
